<section xml:id="sec-mod8-study-guide">
  <title>Companion Reading and Study Guide</title>
  <p>Reading: Zorn, Sections 5.1<ndash/>5.4. Estimated time: 65 minutes.</p>
    <p>
      This guide accompanies four sections of Zorn's <pubtitle>Understanding
      Real Analysis</pubtitle>. Section 5.1 introduces partitions and Darboux
      sums. Section 5.2 develops the integrability criterion. Section 5.3
      identifies the main classes of integrable functions (continuous and
      monotone). Section 5.4 proves the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read each section before working its questions. The recurring theme
      is the integrability criterion: a function is Riemann integrable if
      and only if for every <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> there is a partition
      <m>P</m> with <m>U(f,P) - L(f,P) \lt \varepsilon</m>. Every positive
      result in this module is proved by exhibiting such a partition.
    </p>
  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTION 5.1: PARTITIONS AND DARBOUX SUMS
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod8-sg-51-intro">
    <title>Section 5.1: Partitions and Darboux Sums</title>
    <p>
      Read Zorn's definitions of partition, Darboux sums, and upper and
      lower integrals carefully. The key notational distinction: <m>M_i =
      \sup\{f(x) : x \in [x_{i-1}, x_i]\}</m> is the supremum (not a
      sample value), and <m>m_i = \inf\{f(x) : x \in [x_{i-1}, x_i]\}</m>
      is the infimum. These always exist when <m>f</m> is bounded.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-sg-51-1">
    <title>Partitions and Mesh</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Define a partition <m>P</m> of <m>[a,b]</m> and the mesh
        <m>\|P\|</m>. For <m>P = \{0, 1/3, 2/3, 1\}</m> of <m>[0,1]</m>,
        compute <m>\|P\|</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        A <term>partition</term> of <m>[a,b]</m> is a finite set
        <m>P = \{x_0, x_1, \ldots, x_n\}</m> with
        <m>a = x_0 \lt x_1 \lt \cdots \lt x_n = b</m>. The <term>mesh</term> is
        <m>\|P\| = \max_{1 \le i \le n} \Delta x_i</m> where
        <m>\Delta x_i = x_i - x_{i-1}</m>. For
        <m>P = \{0, 1/3, 2/3, 1\}</m>, each subinterval has length
        <m>1/3</m>, so <m>\|P\| = 1/3</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-sg-51-2">
    <title>Upper Sums for <m>f(x) = x^2</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For <m>f(x) = x^2</m> on <m>[0,1]</m> with uniform partition
        <m>P_n</m> of <m>n</m> equal subintervals:
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            What is <m>\Delta x = 1/n</m> and what are the partition
            points <m>x_k = k/n</m>?
          </li>
          <li>
            Compute <m>M_k = \sup f</m> on <m>[x_{k-1}, x_k]</m>.
            Since <m>f</m> is increasing, <m>M_k = x_k^2 = k^2/n^2</m>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Write <m>U(f, P_n) = \sum_{k=1}^{n} (k^2/n^2)(1/n)</m>
            and use the formula
            <m>\sum_{k=1}^{n} k^2 = n(n+1)(2n+1)/6</m> to obtain
            <m>U(f, P_n) = (n+1)(2n+1)/(6n^2)</m>.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>\Delta x = 1/n</m>; <m>x_k = k/n</m> for
        <m>k = 0, 1, \ldots, n</m>.
        (b) Since <m>x^2</m> is increasing on <m>[0,1]</m>, the supremum
        on <m>[(k-1)/n, k/n]</m> is attained at the right endpoint:
        <m>M_k = (k/n)^2 = k^2/n^2</m>.
        (c)
        <md>
          U(f, P_n)
          = \sum_{k=1}^{n} \frac{k^2}{n^2} \cdot \frac{1}{n}
          = \frac{1}{n^3} \sum_{k=1}^{n} k^2
          = \frac{1}{n^3} \cdot \frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6}
          = \frac{(n+1)(2n+1)}{6n^2}.
        </md>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTION 5.2: INTEGRABILITY CRITERION
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod8-sg-52-intro">
    <title>Section 5.2: The Integrability Criterion</title>
    <p>
      The integrability criterion is the workhorse of this module.
      Read Zorn's statement and proof carefully. Every positive
      integrability result (continuous functions, monotone
      functions) is proved by exhibiting a partition that
      makes <m>U(f,P) - L(f,P)</m> small.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-sg-52-1">
    <title>Stating the Integrability Criterion</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State the Riemann integrability criterion precisely. Why is
        <m>U(f,P) - L(f,P)</m> sometimes called the
        <q>oscillation sum</q>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        A bounded function <m>f</m> on <m>[a,b]</m> is Riemann
        integrable if and only if for every <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>
        there exists a partition <m>P</m> of <m>[a,b]</m> such that
        <m>U(f,P) - L(f,P) \lt \varepsilon</m>.
        The quantity <m>U(f,P) - L(f,P) = \sum (M_i - m_i)\Delta x_i</m>
        is the oscillation sum because <m>M_i - m_i</m> measures the
        oscillation of <m>f</m> on the <m>i</m>th subinterval:
        the spread between the largest and smallest values of <m>f</m>
        on that piece. When the total oscillation (weighted by subinterval
        length) can be made arbitrarily small, the function is integrable.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-sg-52-2">
    <title>The Dirichlet Function</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For the Dirichlet function
        <m>f(x) = 1</m> if <m>x \in \Q</m>,
        <m>f(x) = 0</m> if <m>x \notin \Q</m>:
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            What is <m>M_k = \sup f</m> on any subinterval
            <m>[x_{k-1}, x_k]</m>? (Recall that the rationals are
            dense in <m>\R</m>.)
          </li>
          <li>
            What is <m>m_k = \inf f</m> on any subinterval?
            (Recall that the irrationals are also dense in <m>\R</m>.)
          </li>
          <li>
            Compute <m>U(f,P) - L(f,P)</m> for any partition <m>P</m>
            of <m>[0,1]</m>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Is the Dirichlet function Riemann integrable on
            <m>[0,1]</m>?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) Since <m>\Q</m> is dense in <m>\R</m>, every subinterval
        contains rationals, so <m>M_k = 1</m>.
        (b) Since <m>\R \setminus \Q</m> is dense in <m>\R</m>, every
        subinterval contains irrationals, so <m>m_k = 0</m>.
        (c) <m>U(f,P) - L(f,P) = \sum (1 - 0)\Delta x_k = 1</m> for
        any partition of <m>[0,1]</m>.
        (d) No: since <m>U(f,P) - L(f,P) = 1</m> for every partition,
        the integrability criterion fails (take <m>\varepsilon = 1/2</m>,
        for instance). The upper integral equals <m>1</m> and the lower integral
        equals <m>0</m>, so they are not equal and <m>f</m> is not Riemann
        integrable.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTION 5.3: CLASSES OF INTEGRABLE FUNCTIONS
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod8-sg-53-intro">
    <title>Section 5.3: Classes of Integrable Functions</title>
    <p>
      Zorn establishes two main classes: continuous functions (using
      uniform continuity) and monotone functions (using a telescoping
      argument). Read both proofs, paying attention to where each uses
      special properties of the function class.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-sg-53-1">
    <title>Continuous Implies Integrable</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Sketch the proof that if <m>f</m> is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m>,
        then <m>f</m> is Riemann integrable on <m>[a,b]</m>. Which
        fact from <xref ref="ch-mod6"/> is used and where?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        The key tool is the Heine<ndash/>Cantor Theorem
        (<xref ref="ex-mod6-sg-34-3"/> states it; see Zorn or MAT 5610
        for a proof): a
        continuous function on a closed bounded interval is uniformly
        continuous. Given <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, apply uniform
        continuity to get <m>\delta \gt 0</m> such that
        <m>|x - y| \lt \delta \Rightarrow |f(x) - f(y)| \lt
        \varepsilon/(2(b-a))</m>. Choose any partition <m>P</m> with
        mesh <m>\|P\| \lt \delta</m>. On each subinterval
        <m>[x_{i-1}, x_i]</m>, any two points are within <m>\delta</m>
        of each other, so <m>M_i - m_i \leq \varepsilon/(2(b-a))</m>
        by uniform continuity. (Taking a supremum can turn
        <m>\lt</m> into <m>\leq</m>; the factor <m>2</m> leaves room
        for that.) Then
        <md>
          U(f,P) - L(f,P)
          = \sum_{i=1}^{n} (M_i - m_i)\Delta x_i
          \leq \frac{\varepsilon}{2(b-a)} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \Delta x_i
          = \frac{\varepsilon}{2} \lt \varepsilon.
        </md>
        By the integrability criterion, <m>f</m> is integrable. Uniform
        continuity is used to control <m>M_i - m_i</m>. Continuity at
        each point separately gives a <m>\delta</m> that may depend on
        the point, and this argument needs one <m>\delta</m> that works
        everywhere on <m>[a,b]</m> simultaneously; on a closed bounded
        interval, the Heine<ndash/>Cantor Theorem supplies it.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-sg-53-2">
    <title>Monotone Implies Integrable</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that a monotone function on <m>[a,b]</m> is Riemann
        integrable.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Assume without loss of generality that <m>f</m> is
            increasing on <m>[a,b]</m>. For the uniform partition
            <m>P_n</m> with <m>n</m> equal subintervals of width
            <m>(b-a)/n</m>, show that
            <md>
              U(f,P_n) - L(f,P_n)
              = \frac{b-a}{n}\sum_{k=1}^{n}[f(x_k) - f(x_{k-1})]
              = \frac{(b-a)(f(b) - f(a))}{n},
            </md>
            using the telescoping nature of the sum and the fact that
            <m>M_k = f(x_k)</m> and <m>m_k = f(x_{k-1})</m> for an
            increasing function.
          </li>
          <li>
            Apply the integrability criterion: given <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>,
            choose <m>n \gt (b-a)(f(b) - f(a))/\varepsilon</m> to conclude.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) For increasing <m>f</m>, on the <m>k</m>th subinterval
        <m>[x_{k-1}, x_k]</m> the supremum is attained at the right
        endpoint and the infimum at the left: <m>M_k = f(x_k)</m> and
        <m>m_k = f(x_{k-1})</m>. With uniform partition width
        <m>\Delta x = (b-a)/n</m>:
        <md>
          U(f,P_n) - L(f,P_n)
          = \frac{b-a}{n} \sum_{k=1}^{n} [f(x_k) - f(x_{k-1})]
          = \frac{b-a}{n} [f(b) - f(a)],
        </md>
        since the sum telescopes.
        (b) Given <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, choose
        <m>n \gt (b-a)(f(b)-f(a))/\varepsilon</m>
        (or <m>n = 1</m> if <m>f(b) = f(a)</m>). Then
        <m>U(f,P_n) - L(f,P_n) = (b-a)(f(b)-f(a))/n \lt \varepsilon</m>.
        By the integrability criterion, <m>f</m> is integrable.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       SECTION 5.4: THE FUNDAMENTAL THEOREM OF CALCULUS
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod8-sg-54-intro">
    <title>Section 5.4: The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</title>
    <p>
      Read both parts of the FTC carefully. Part 1 requires
      differentiating a function defined by an integral; Part 2 uses
      Part 1 together with the <xref ref="ch-mod7"/> corollary that a function
      with zero derivative on an interval is constant.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod8-sg-54-props">
    <title>Properties of the Integral Used in the FTC Proofs</title>
    <p>
      The proofs in this section, and in the rest of the module, use
      the following properties of the integral. Let <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> be
      Riemann integrable on <m>[a,b]</m>.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Linearity.</em> For constants <m>c</m> and <m>d</m>, the
            function <m>cf + dg</m> is integrable on <m>[a,b]</m> and
            <m>\int_a^b (cf + dg) = c\int_a^b f + d\int_a^b g</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Monotonicity.</em> If <m>f(x) \leq g(x)</m> for all
            <m>x \in [a,b]</m>, then <m>\int_a^b f \leq \int_a^b g</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Additivity over intervals.</em> If <m>a \lt q \lt b</m>,
            then <m>f</m> is integrable on <m>[a,q]</m> and on
            <m>[q,b]</m>, and
            <m>\int_a^b f = \int_a^q f + \int_q^b f</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      Read Zorn's statements and proofs of these properties
      (&#xa7;5.1<ndash/>5.4). A second source is Rudin,
      <pubtitle>Principles of Mathematical Analysis</pubtitle>,
      3rd edition, Theorem 6.12.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Constant functions.</em> If <m>f(x) = c</m> for all
      <m>x \in [a,b]</m>, then for every partition <m>P</m> of
      <m>[a,b]</m> we have <m>M_i = m_i = c</m> on every subinterval, so
      <m>U(f,P) = L(f,P) = \sum c\,\Delta x_i = c(b-a)</m>. Hence the
      upper and lower integrals both equal <m>c(b-a)</m>, so <m>f</m> is
      integrable and
      <md>\int_a^b c\,dx = c(b-a).</md>
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Orientation convention.</em> So far <m>\int_a^b f</m> has meant
      <m>a \lt b</m>. The FTC proofs also need integrals such as
      <m>\int_x^{x+h} f</m> with <m>h \lt 0</m>, and
      <m>\int_a^a f</m>. Define <m>\int_a^a f = 0</m> and, for
      <m>a \lt b</m>,
      <md>\int_b^a f = -\int_a^b f.</md>
      With this convention,
      <m>\int_p^q f + \int_q^r f = \int_p^r f</m> for any points
      <m>p, q, r</m>, in any order, of an interval on which <m>f</m> is
      integrable. Each of these integrals is defined because, by
      additivity, <m>f</m> is integrable on every closed subinterval of
      that interval. For example, if <m>q \lt p \lt r</m>, additivity
      gives <m>\int_q^r f = \int_q^p f + \int_p^r f</m>, so
      <m>\int_p^q f + \int_q^r f = -\int_q^p f + \int_q^p f + \int_p^r f
      = \int_p^r f</m>. The other orders, and the cases in which two of
      the points coincide, are checked the same way.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-sg-54-1">
    <title>FTC Part 1: Hypotheses and Statement</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        State FTC Part 1 precisely, including all hypotheses. What
        does it mean to say that <m>F(x) = \int_a^x f(t)\,dt</m>
        is differentiable at <m>x</m>? (You must compute
        <m>F'(x)</m> from the limit definition of the derivative.)
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <em>FTC Part 1.</em> Let <m>f</m> be continuous on <m>[a,b]</m>
        and define <m>F(x) = \int_a^x f(t)\,dt</m> for <m>x \in [a,b]</m>.
        Then <m>F</m> is differentiable on <m>(a,b)</m> and
        <m>F'(x) = f(x)</m> for all <m>x \in (a,b)</m>.
        To say <m>F</m> is differentiable at <m>x</m> means that the
        limit
        <md>
          F'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{F(x+h) - F(x)}{h}
                = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{1}{h}\int_x^{x+h} f(t)\,dt
        </md>
        exists and equals <m>f(x)</m>. (The second expression uses
        additivity and the orientation convention from
        <xref ref="par-mod8-sg-54-props" text="custom">Properties of the
        Integral Used in the FTC Proofs</xref>, so it is valid for
        <m>h</m> of either sign.)
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-sg-54-2">
    <title>FTC Part 2: Statement and Proof</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            State FTC Part 2 precisely, listing all hypotheses.
          </li>
          <li>
            Prove FTC Part 2 using FTC Part 1 as a lemma: if
            <m>F' = f</m> on <m>[a,b]</m>, define
            <m>G(x) = \int_a^x f</m>. By Part 1, <m>G' = f = F'</m>,
            so <m>(G - F)' = 0</m>. By the <xref ref="ch-mod7"/> corollary (a
            function with zero derivative on an interval is constant),
            <m>G - F = C</m> for some constant. Evaluate at <m>x = a</m>
            to find <m>C</m>, then at <m>x = b</m> to conclude.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <em>FTC Part 2.</em> Let <m>f</m> be continuous on
        <m>[a,b]</m> and let <m>F</m> be differentiable on <m>[a,b]</m>
        with <m>F' = f</m>. Then
        <md>\int_a^b f(x)\,dx = F(b) - F(a).</md>
        (b) Define <m>G(x) = \int_a^x f(t)\,dt</m>. By FTC Part 1,
        <m>G'(x) = f(x) = F'(x)</m> for all <m>x \in (a,b)</m>. Hence
        <m>(G - F)'(x) = 0</m> on <m>(a,b)</m>. By the <xref ref="ch-mod7"/> Mean Value Theorem
        corollary (<xref ref="ex-mod7-sg-43-3"/>), there is a constant
        <m>C \in \R</m> with <m>G(x) - F(x) = C</m> for all
        <m>x \in (a,b)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The corollary says nothing about the endpoints, so we extend
        the equation to <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> by continuity. Since
        <m>F</m> is differentiable on <m>[a,b]</m>, it is continuous on
        <m>[a,b]</m> (<xref ref="ex-mod7-we-2"/>; at <m>a</m> and
        <m>b</m> the same argument works with one-sided limits). For
        <m>G</m>: <m>f</m> is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m>, so it is
        bounded there by the Extreme Value Theorem
        (<xref ref="ex-mod6-sg-32-3"/>); say <m>|f(t)| \leq K</m> for all
        <m>t \in [a,b]</m>. If <m>a \leq y \lt x \leq b</m>, then
        <m>G(x) - G(y) = \int_y^x f(t)\,dt</m> by additivity, and
        monotonicity applied to <m>-K \leq f \leq K</m> on
        <m>[y,x]</m>, with the integral of a constant, gives
        <m>-K(x-y) \leq \int_y^x f(t)\,dt \leq K(x-y)</m>. So
        <m>|G(x) - G(y)| \leq K|x - y|</m> for all
        <m>x, y \in [a,b]</m>, and <m>G</m> is continuous on
        <m>[a,b]</m>. (These properties are in
        <xref ref="par-mod8-sg-54-props" text="custom">Properties of the
        Integral Used in the FTC Proofs</xref>.) Therefore
        <m>G - F</m> is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m> and equal to
        <m>C</m> on <m>(a,b)</m>, so
        <md>G(a) - F(a) = \lim_{x \to a^+} \bigl(G(x) - F(x)\bigr) = C,</md>
        and in the same way <m>G(b) - F(b) = C</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Evaluating at <m>x = a</m>:
        <m>G(a) - F(a) = 0 - F(a) = -F(a)</m>, since
        <m>G(a) = \int_a^a f = 0</m>; so <m>C = -F(a)</m>.
        Thus <m>G(x) = F(x) - F(a)</m> for all <m>x \in [a,b]</m>.
        Evaluating at <m>x = b</m>:
        <m>\int_a^b f = G(b) = F(b) - F(a)</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

</section>
